      "passage": "India's space programme has entered a new phase. For decades, the country's space agency was primarily associated with launching satellites and developing technologies under considerable financial and technological constraints. Today, however, space technology has become closely connected with communication, weather forecasting, disaster management, agriculture and national security. This expansion of applications has naturally increased expectations from India's space institutions.\n\nYet, greater expectations should not mean that every possible activity must be pursued by the same institution. A public organisation such as ISRO has a distinctive role in building capabilities that may not immediately attract private investment but can produce substantial benefits for society. Its strength lies not merely in launching rockets but also in developing technologies, creating scientific expertise and addressing problems that have wider national importance.\n\nThe growing participation of private companies in the space sector is therefore not necessarily a threat to public institutions. On the contrary, it can allow the government space programme to concentrate on areas requiring long-term research and strategic attention, while private enterprises take up commercially viable activities. Such a division, however, requires a clear policy framework. Without it, competition between institutions may create confusion rather than efficiency.\n\nThe challenge, therefore, is not to choose between the public and private sectors, but to determine what each can do best. India needs an ecosystem in which public institutions provide scientific leadership and technological foundations, while private companies contribute innovation, investment and speed. If this balance is maintained, the expansion of the space sector can serve not only commercial interests but also broader developmental goals.",
