      "title": "When Cities Become Hotter Than Their Surroundings",
      "difficulty": "medium_plus",
      "passage": "A city is not merely a collection of buildings and roads. The materials from which it is constructed can fundamentally alter its local climate. Concrete, asphalt and other dense surfaces absorb and retain heat during the day, releasing it slowly after sunset. At the same time, the replacement of vegetation with buildings reduces the natural cooling that occurs when plants release water into the atmosphere. The result is what scientists describe as the urban heat island effect: cities can remain considerably warmer than nearby rural areas, particularly at night.\n\nThis phenomenon is not simply an environmental curiosity. Persistent nighttime heat can prevent the human body from recovering from daytime exposure, especially during prolonged periods of extreme weather. The burden is not distributed equally either. People living in poorly ventilated homes, working outdoors, or lacking access to reliable cooling may face substantially greater risks than those who can afford air-conditioning and better-insulated housing.\n\nUrban planning, therefore, has consequences that extend beyond aesthetics. Planting trees, creating shaded public spaces and protecting existing vegetation can reduce exposure to heat. Yet such measures should not be treated as universal solutions. Trees require water and maintenance, while poorly planned planting can sometimes obstruct airflow or place additional pressure on scarce resources. Similarly, air-conditioning can protect individuals indoors but, when widely used, increases electricity demand and may contribute to outdoor heat through the release of waste heat.\n\nThe more difficult task is consequently to design cities in which protection from heat does not depend entirely on an individual's ability to purchase it. This requires combining several relatively modest interventions—better building design, reflective surfaces, vegetation, accessible public spaces and early warnings during extreme heat. No single measure is likely to eliminate the problem. The objective should instead be to reduce vulnerability across the urban population.\n\nA heat-resilient city, in this sense, is not necessarily one that never becomes hot. It is one in which high temperatures are less likely to become a serious threat to people's health and livelihoods.",
